---
<Layout title="Circuits">
    <div slot="navtree">
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush py-0">
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Overview'>Circuits Overview</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Simulator'>Interactive Circuit &amp; Current Lab</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#WhatAreCircuits'>What are Circuits?</a></li>
            <li class='list-group-item py-0'><a class='text-decoration-none subsection' href='#Filtering'>Using Circuits for Filtering</a></li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <Section title="Circuits" />

    <SubSection title="Circuits Overview" id="Overview">
        <p>
            Circuits traditionally consist of wires, power/current sources, resistors, capacitors, and inductors, and can be used for a vast variety of purposes. Due to the ways that these components affect voltage and current in a circuit, circuits are a useful way for us to physically interact with signals without needing digital filtering or circuit analysis. We have certain building blocks, and can construct circuits based on the parameters we need, which are current, voltage, and resistance. We can use this to solve most problems and construct physical filters. These allow us to avoid digital components and filters as they are often used to replicate the effect of physical components computationally. Using simple circuit components can reduce the footprint and the size scale of the device for portability and reduces energy consumption.
        </p>

        <div class="card p-3 my-3 border bg-light-subtle shadow-sm">
            <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-2">Example: Passive RC Low Pass Filter</h6>
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-center my-2">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 460 170" class="img-fluid rounded border bg-white p-2" style="max-width: 480px; width: 100%; height: auto;">
                    <!-- Input Source -->
                    <circle cx="50" cy="85" r="22" fill="none" stroke="#0d6efd" stroke-width="2.5"/>
                    <path d="M 38 85 Q 44 72 50 85 T 62 85" fill="none" stroke="#0d6efd" stroke-width="2"/>
                    <text x="50" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#0d6efd">Vin</text>

                    <!-- Top wire from source to resistor -->
                    <line x1="50" y1="63" x2="50" y2="40" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>
                    <line x1="50" y1="40" x2="130" y2="40" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>

