                    <text x="230" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#0284c7">Capacitor, C</text>
                    <line x1="300" y1="88" x2="300" y2="130" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>

                    <!-- Bottom wire -->
                    <line x1="50" y1="107" x2="50" y2="130" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>
                    <line x1="50" y1="130" x2="410" y2="130" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>
                    <circle cx="300" cy="130" r="3.5" fill="#334155"/>
                    <circle cx="410" cy="130" r="4" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#198754" stroke-width="2"/>

                    <!-- Ground at bottom -->
                    <line x1="300" y1="130" x2="300" y2="145" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2"/>
                    <line x1="288" y1="145" x2="312" y2="145" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5"/>
                    <line x1="292" y1="150" x2="308" y2="150" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2"/>
                    <line x1="296" y1="155" x2="304" y2="155" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="1.5"/>

                    <!-- Output voltage label -->
                    <text x="435" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#198754">Vout</text>
                    <path d="M 418 48 L 418 122" fill="none" stroke="#198754" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3,2"/>
                </svg>
            </div>
            <small class="text-muted text-center d-block">Figure: Passive first-order RC low-pass filter topology.</small>
        </div>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="Interactive Circuit &amp; Current Lab" id="Simulator">
        <p>
            Use the interactive circuit simulator and calculator below to investigate how altering physical component values (<InlineEquation equation="R"/> and <InlineEquation equation="C"/>) changes total circuit impedance (<InlineEquation equation="|Z|"/>), alters instantaneous current draw <InlineEquation equation="i(t)"/>, and shapes the output voltage <InlineEquation equation="V_{out}"/>. Switch between Low-Pass, High-Pass, and Resistive Divider modes in real time:
        </p>
        <CircuitSimulator />
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="What are circuits?" id="WhatAreCircuits">
        <p>
            Circuits traditionally consist of wires, power/current sources, resistors, capacitors, and inductors, and can be used for a vast variety of purposes. In bioengineering, circuits provide the hardware foundation for acquiring biopotentials (such as ECG, EMG, and EEG) and physically filtering out electrical noise before digital conversion.
        </p>

        <CalloutCard title="Cross-Course Connection: Dr. Jenny Amos on Circuits & Conservation Laws">
            <p class="mb-2">
                Circuit analysis in biomedical engineering relies fundamentally on the conservation of charge and energy. To review the core circuit elements, voltage-current relationships, and Kirchhoff’s Laws taught by <strong>Dr. Jenny Amos</strong> in BIOE 202, explore these companion reference pages:
            </p>
            <div class="row g-2 mt-2">
                <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
                    <a href="/cons/conservation_of_charge" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm w-100 text-start d-flex align-items-center gap-2 py-2 px-3 shadow-sm h-100">
                        <i class="bi bi-lightning-charge-fill text-warning fs-4"></i>
                        <div>
                            <div class="fw-bold">Conservation of Charge (BIOE 202)</div>
                            <small class="text-muted">Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL), Voltage Law (KVL), and Resistors</small>
                        </div>
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div class="col-12 col-md-6">
                    <a href="/cons/conservation_and_accounting_laws" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm w-100 text-start d-flex align-items-center gap-2 py-2 px-3 shadow-sm h-100">
                        <i class="bi bi-journal-bookmark-fill text-primary fs-4"></i>
                        <div>
                            <div class="fw-bold">Conservation &amp; Accounting Laws</div>
                            <small class="text-muted">Universal system boundaries and conservation statements</small>
                        </div>
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-3 text-muted small">
                <i class="bi bi-person-badge text-secondary me-1"></i> Taught by <strong>Dr. Jenny Amos</strong> in BIOE 202 (Conservation Principles). Explore her course curriculum on the <a href="/cons" class="fw-bold text-decoration-none">Conservation Principles Portal</a> or visit <a href="https://bioengineering.illinois.edu/people/jamos" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="fw-bold text-decoration-none">Dr. Amos's Faculty Profile</a>.
            </div>
        </CalloutCard>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Below is a table of basic elements in a circuit and their purpose:
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive my-3">
            <table class="table table-bordered table-hover align-middle shadow-sm">
